 [{:collection :canonical-tasks,
   :id
   "runtime/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/implement-explicit-family-selection-and-no-runtime-rejection",
   :field-path [:acceptance],
   :before-present? true,
   :before
   "Actual service link/load inspection for every claimed profile/target and forbidden-service rejection. R1/R2/R4: explicit family selection and compatible composition; linked/generated/delegated/external/forbidden service records consumed by backend, package, observability, conformance and self-hosting tools; runtime-none entry/reset/interrupt/ABI, sections, memory map, alignment and required target stack evidence; generated checks and panic helpers with provenance. Reject hidden GC, allocator, scheduler, reflection, eval, host IO and hosted-profile leakage; unsupported dynamic forms reject unless eliminated with evidence. Dependencies: Lower supplies target ABI/artifacts, Legality supplies profile facts, and Build supplies package policy; no product ISA or platform choice is made here.",
   :after
   "Actual service link/load inspection for every claimed profile/target and forbidden-service rejection. R1/R2/R4: explicit family selection and compatible composition; linked/generated/delegated/external/forbidden service records consumed by backend, package, observability, conformance and self-hosting tools; runtime-none entry/reset/interrupt/ABI, sections, memory map, alignment and required target stack evidence; generated checks and panic helpers with provenance. Reject hidden GC, allocator, scheduler, reflection, eval, host IO and hosted-profile leakage; unsupported dynamic forms reject unless eliminated with evidence. Dependencies: Lower supplies target ABI/artifacts, Legality supplies profile facts, and Build supplies package policy; no product ISA or platform choice is made here. Residual criterion runtime/family-composition: For each claimed profile/target pair, resolve the selected combination of R1 runtime families under profile compatibility and package policy, emitting the family-selection record and linked/generated/delegated/external/forbidden service table. Exercise an allowed combination and reject incompatible combinations, undeclared providers and hidden universal-runtime dependencies. Every selected service and typed host adapter retains its effects, capability hook or applicable static proof, startup/failure model and provenance; listing families without checking the combination is not acceptance. Residual criterion runtime/manifest-consumption: Have backend, package, observability, conformance and self-hosting consumers read the emitted runtime manifest and cross-check it against the actual selected service/helper/provider graph. Emit consumer agreement evidence for family/profile/target, service classification, linked support objects, generated helpers, delegated typed adapters, external providers, forbidden services, capability enforcement hooks or static proofs, startup/initialization/cleanup/failure policy and runtime diagnostic schema/provenance. Reject an absent/unreadable manifest, omitted or contradictory service/provider, hidden forbidden dependency, missing enforcement hook/proof or lost artifact/source-origin edge. A manifest link or producer-only record cannot establish consumer integration. Native backend integration includes C and LLVM artifacts for the claimed target matrix; fixture inspection alone does not claim those targets implemented.",
   :source-sha256
   "51b68ffda3eea2e859934d831db59385cd151baaac00a3c946b8d0087aae4de6"}
  {:collection :canonical-tasks,
   :id
   "runtime/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/finish-managednative-execution-startup-panic-and-cleanup",
   :field-path [:acceptance],
   :before-present? true,
   :before
   "Executed startup/failure/resource fixtures and manifest-to-service agreement; before user code, validate selected provider existence, artifact versions, grant scopes, compiled contracts, profile/target compatibility and enabled safety/audit requirements; reject a missing required provider unless a declared fallback applies, with positive fallback and negative mismatch fixtures under [L15 runtime providers](../../docs/phase-01-core-language/025-l15-capability-provider-specification.md) . R1/R3/R4: typed host adapters with effects, capabilities, taint, error and source-map contracts; selected host/version/module/collection/dynamic-state manifests; startup, initialization, handoff and teardown order; explicit panic/trap/abort/unwind/result policy; host null/undefined conversion, exception and rejected-promise translation; Gravity equality/hashing/serialization conformance; deterministic linear cleanup independent of process exit or GC. Reject undeclared helper effects, untranslated/null foreign values and GC-only linear cleanup; debug services must be absent or release-policy marked. Dependencies: Lower supplies ABI and source maps, Legality supplies legal services, and Auth supplies grants; selected compiler services do not close all managed/native targets. P4: execute deterministic cleanup for linear host resources despite GC; managed objects remain GC-managed and only an explicit boundary can rely on finalizer timing. Reject GC-only cleanup where deterministic release is required; preserve exactly-once flow evidence from Legality separately from executed terminal operations. Translate host errors through the declared Gravity failure channel with source/generated origins.",
   :after
   "Executed startup/failure/resource fixtures and manifest-to-service agreement; before user code, validate selected provider existence, artifact versions, grant scopes, compiled contracts, profile/target compatibility and enabled safety/audit requirements; reject a missing required provider unless a declared fallback applies, with positive fallback and negative mismatch fixtures under [L15 runtime providers](../../docs/phase-01-core-language/025-l15-capability-provider-specification.md) . R1/R3/R4: typed host adapters with effects, capabilities, taint, error and source-map contracts; selected host/version/module/collection/dynamic-state manifests; startup, initialization, handoff and teardown order; explicit panic/trap/abort/unwind/result policy; host null/undefined conversion, exception and rejected-promise translation; Gravity equality/hashing/serialization conformance; deterministic linear cleanup independent of process exit or GC. Reject undeclared helper effects, untranslated/null foreign values and GC-only linear cleanup; debug services must be absent or release-policy marked. Dependencies: Lower supplies ABI and source maps, Legality supplies legal services, and Auth supplies grants; selected compiler services do not close all managed/native targets. P4: execute deterministic cleanup for linear host resources despite GC; managed objects remain GC-managed and only an explicit boundary can rely on finalizer timing. Reject GC-only cleanup where deterministic release is required; preserve exactly-once flow evidence from Legality separately from executed terminal operations. Translate host errors through the declared Gravity failure channel with source/generated origins. Residual criterion runtime/debug-service-selection: Select debug services explicitly for each native/managed runtime and release policy. Show a debug artifact and release artifact whose service/helper manifests expose exactly the selected stack/allocation/check/trace hooks; debug-only services are absent in release unless policy explicitly allows and marks them. Preserve source/check/allocation/provider/lifetime/release identity and proof-elision metadata. Stack diagnostics are capability-neutral unless an external lookup is enabled: reject undeclared filesystem, environment, network or symbol-server access, unmarked debug services and lost source/provenance; required safety checks cannot disappear with debug instrumentation. Provider debug manifests retain profile support, allocation effects, layout/alignment, failure/lifetime/deallocation strategy and unsafe boundaries; traces retain allocation site, resource ID and release site, including selected borrow-debug checks. Enforce sink grants and redact secrets, credentials, prompts, model outputs, user/tainted inputs, raw memory, environment values, package tokens, database rows and deployment identifiers; local logging permission cannot authorize network telemetry. Residual criterion runtime/managed-collection-semantics: For each claimed managed host/version and collection implementation, execute Gravity-observable value/collection fixtures for host representation, nullability, equality, hashing, serialization, mutability, identity and taint. Emit the managed-value/collection manifest and source-bound comparison results for persistent values adapted from host collections or implemented by Gravity. Reject observable host equality/hash/serialization/mutation behavior that diverges from the declared Gravity contract, unchecked nulls and a collection implementation missing from the host manifest; host convenience cannot define portable core meaning. Residual criterion runtime/managed-dynamic-state: Manifest dynamic variables, thread-local values, async context, namespace loading, REPL state and hot reload for the selected host/version/module system. Show declared binding/context behavior through thread and async boundaries and namespace/session changes, retaining source maps, taint, error translation and resource policy. Reject unmanifested state, ambient host reflection/eval/dynamic import/package globals without effects/grants and hosted dynamic state leaking into a lower profile without a legal facade/artifact. Session state influencing build artifacts is captured or nonhermetic, not an implicit capability. Residual criterion runtime/managed-null-conversion: At each claimed host adapter, manifest host/Gravity representation and nullability and convert host null or undefined through the selected Option, Result, checked-wrapper or opaque-foreign-value contract. Execute null, undefined and non-null fixtures for that declared boundary, retaining taint, errors and source/generated-origin diagnostics. Reject unchecked host null/undefined entering a safe Gravity value, a conversion inconsistent with its Gravity type or a missing adapter/null policy; an opaque foreign value does not authorize unchecked access or leakage into lower profiles. Alternative conversion policies are conditional choices, not a requirement to apply every conversion to one value. Exception/null boundary fixtures also reject untranslated host exceptions or rejected promises and preserve the declared Gravity error/panic/effect channel.",
   :source-sha256
   "dcc3026996f94e0e5c3d35652c243ab6ef151606ffb9d4dc79a752fbc99b909c"}
  {:collection :canonical-tasks,
   :id
   "runtime/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/implement-memory-concurrency-and-typed-ffi-boundaries",
   :field-path [:acceptance],
   :before-present? true,
   :before
   "Allocation, lifetime, transfer, race and foreign-failure conformance with explicit providers. Execute the [SAFE2-SAFE8 contracts](../../docs/phase-02-safety/README.md): declared allocation and cleanup failures, partial initialization cleanup, region/arena generation and borrow checks, release through the correct allocator/provider, callback lifetime/thread/reentrancy/release and foreign error/exception/promise translation. Reject retained borrows, post-reset access, missing FFI declarations, unsupported atomics and unsynchronized mutable access; validate immutable sharing, lock cleanup, actor/channel transfer and declared backpressure/cancellation. Inspect actual checks and audit records, with no hidden GC or host-thread fallback. . R3/R5/R6/R10: allocation/deallocation/provider manifests, no-allocation and region/arena escape rejection, linear acquire/transfer/consume ledgers, device address-space/transfer/synchronization/lifetime checks, audited raw-memory wrappers, and agreement of retained checks with exact proof-elision records; atomics with target memory order/scope/alignment, structured task parent or explicit lifecycle owner, cancellation cleanup and failure propagation, actor/channel schemas with ordering/backpressure/transfer policy; FFI symbol/loading policy, ABI/layout conformance, null/handle/lifetime/ownership/error adapters and callbacks re-entering capability/taint/thread-affinity/scheduler checks. Reject mismatched providers, races, orphan tasks, unsupported atomics, schemaless messages, raw extern safe calls and invalid callback entry. Dependencies: ownership and Legality supply compile-time facts, Lower supplies target layouts, Auth supplies grants, and distributed execution supplies replay policy; runtime debug checks cannot substitute for compile-time safety. P4/P5: host-object/native FFI records bind runtime/version/symbol, Gravity and foreign types, rooting/ownership/lifetime, nullability, failure mapping, affinity, ABI, effects/grants and safe wrappers. Insufficient host metadata permits opaque provider-only handles, not unrestricted manipulation; raw native imports are not directly safe-callable. Exercise positive boundaries and missing metadata, ungranted call, invalid lifetime and untranslated failure rejection.",
   :after
   "Allocation, lifetime, transfer, race and foreign-failure conformance with explicit providers. Execute the [SAFE2-SAFE8 contracts](../../docs/phase-02-safety/README.md): declared allocation and cleanup failures, partial initialization cleanup, region/arena generation and borrow checks, release through the correct allocator/provider, callback lifetime/thread/reentrancy/release and foreign error/exception/promise translation. Reject retained borrows, post-reset access, missing FFI declarations, unsupported atomics and unsynchronized mutable access; validate immutable sharing, lock cleanup, actor/channel transfer and declared backpressure/cancellation. Inspect actual checks and audit records, with no hidden GC or host-thread fallback. . R3/R5/R6/R10: allocation/deallocation/provider manifests, no-allocation and region/arena escape rejection, linear acquire/transfer/consume ledgers, device address-space/transfer/synchronization/lifetime checks, audited raw-memory wrappers, and agreement of retained checks with exact proof-elision records; atomics with target memory order/scope/alignment, structured task parent or explicit lifecycle owner, cancellation cleanup and failure propagation, actor/channel schemas with ordering/backpressure/transfer policy; FFI symbol/loading policy, ABI/layout conformance, null/handle/lifetime/ownership/error adapters and callbacks re-entering capability/taint/thread-affinity/scheduler checks. Reject mismatched providers, races, orphan tasks, unsupported atomics, schemaless messages, raw extern safe calls and invalid callback entry. Dependencies: ownership and Legality supply compile-time facts, Lower supplies target layouts, Auth supplies grants, and distributed execution supplies replay policy; runtime debug checks cannot substitute for compile-time safety. P4/P5: host-object/native FFI records bind runtime/version/symbol, Gravity and foreign types, rooting/ownership/lifetime, nullability, failure mapping, affinity, ABI, effects/grants and safe wrappers. Insufficient host metadata permits opaque provider-only handles, not unrestricted manipulation; raw native imports are not directly safe-callable. Exercise positive boundaries and missing metadata, ungranted call, invalid lifetime and untranslated failure rejection. Residual criterion runtime/foreign-symbol-resolution: Resolve a declared foreign symbol and dynamically load an admitted library/module only under package/deployment policy, the named provider/library/version identity and required effects/grants. Emit the binding, symbol-resolution and generated-adapter records tied to target ABI, calling convention, layout/alignment and source/unsafe audit. Positive fixtures resolve the admitted identity through its safe wrapper/provider; negative fixtures reject wrong/missing symbols, mismatched ABI/layout/provider identity and ungranted or policy-denied dynamic loading. Resolution/loading does not bypass nullability, lifetime/ownership, callback affinity/taint or foreign error conversion contracts. Preserve the delivered split between resolution and loading as two criteria within this same task, without accepting an alias or retiring their history.",
   :source-sha256
   "967d1379aa661f6f8b9831aa784156e08b8a620927eae641db4467b1243108a6"}
  {:collection :canonical-tasks,
   :id
   "runtime/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/connect-distributedai-replay-and-interactive-execution",
   :field-path [:acceptance],
   :before-present? true,
   :before
   "Recorded nondeterminism, replay equality, no undeclared duplicated external effect, REPL grant denial. R6/R7/R8/R9: topology/provider/event-log/state/message schemas; durable idempotency, timeout/retry/cancellation/failure/compensation records; migration or rejection of replay-incompatible code/topology/schema changes; model/provider/prompt provenance, output and sink validation, policy-recorded provider substitution, typed tool contracts with caller/callee grants and policy-required human review, memory/retrieval privacy/retention/deletion/taint controls and secret redaction; R7/R8 replay-required segments return recorded model/tool/retrieval/memory/review decisions or reject a missing record. R6 concurrency fixtures accept an authorized graph-declared replay-safe repetition where no R7/R8 recorded-output obligation applies; the declaration never grants authority or overrides those recorded-output obligations. Interactive forms and generated code pass the ordinary compiler checks before execution; session manifests, transcripts, evaluated forms, syntax/core/MIR/source snapshots capture build-affecting state or mark nonhermeticity, and hot reload invalidates stale analysis/backend/runtime/package outputs. Reject undeclared repeated external calls, live nondeterministic calls in R7/R8 recorded-output segments even with a replay-safe graph declaration, repetitions without required authority, unvalidated trusted sinks, forbidden-profile eval, hidden interactive IO and stale reload artifacts. Dependencies: Domains supplies agent/workflow contracts, Schema supplies validators/migrations, Build consumes session inputs and invalidation, Auth supplies grants and review policy. Project work/token/call/time/retry allowances are removed admission requirements; actual provider billing/account limits remain external, and semantic failure/cancellation policy remains required. R8 mixed budget spans retain unresolved qualification in the family proposal.. Under [SAFE8/SAFE13](.